#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075061 :tutorial/content "The systems of equations we've encountered up to now have all had *exactly one solution*.\n\nFor example, consider the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=2x + 1 \\\\ y &=1 - x\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nUsing either substitution or elimination, we can show that the solution $(x,y)$ to this system is $(0,1)$. This is the *only* solution, and it tells us the point of intersection between the two corresponding lines.\n\n![](images/bd/bd7edb530e16b853318a3e38785c7b6353c1b54f10ee3c4e2de7da915450f6d9.png)\n\nIn general, systems of equations can have\n\n- *exactly one* solution (like the example above)\n- *infinitely many* solutions\n- *no solutions*\n\nTo determine the number of solutions to a system of equations, we solve it using either substitution or elimination and interpret the result.\n\nLet's look at a system of equations with infinitely many solutions." :tutorial/math-academy-id 9683 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
